<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- Common settings across all projects -->
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    REPRODUCIBLE RESTORE (TD-129).

    Central package management pins every version this repository NAMES; transitive versions still
    floated, so two restores of one commit on two days could produce different assemblies and
    nothing showed it. Each restorable project now carries a committed packages.lock.json that
    records the whole resolved graph, with content hashes.

    RestoreLockedMode is on when CI=true (GitHub Actions sets it) and can be forced with
    -p:RestoreLockedMode=true: a restore whose resolved graph differs from the lock file FAILS
    (NU1004) instead of quietly resolving something else, and a package whose content differs from the recorded hash fails too (NU1403). Locally it stays off, so adding or
    bumping a package regenerates the lock file; commit the result. To regenerate deliberately:
    dotnet restore <project> with the force-evaluate switch

    SCOPE. The net10.0-android and net10.0-ios heads opt out in their own .csproj: they cannot be
    restored without their workloads (NETSDK1147), so no lock file for them can be produced or
    verified on a machine that lacks one. They are the only projects outside the lock; a test
    (BuildHygieneTests) fails when any other project lacks a lock file or opts out.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NUGET AUDIT (TD-55).

    TreatWarningsAsErrors promotes the NU1901-NU1904 vulnerability warnings to errors, so a newly
    published advisory against an UNCHANGED dependency would turn the build red with no code change.
    The audit stays on, and stays visible as warnings, but those four IDs are exempted from
    promotion: advisories are triaged on their own schedule rather than breaking every build. The
    audit is a best-effort network lookup; when the advisory source is unreachable (offline builds)
    NuGet reports NU1900, which is a warning, and is likewise not promoted.
    NuGetAuditMode=all audits transitive packages too; NuGetAuditLevel=low reports every severity.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1900;NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    VERSION IDENTITY (TD-49).

    WHAT WAS WRONG. Nothing anywhere set a version, so MSBuild used its default and every build Pine
    has ever produced stamped `1.0.0`. That string is what `PineRuntimeInfoProvider.AppVersion` shows
    on the diagnostics panel and what `PineLogRecordFormatter` writes into the `version` field of
    every log record. A build number that is the same for every build is not a build number: two
    users on two different binaries filing two different bug reports were indistinguishable, and the
    panel that iteration 12 built specifically so that its figures would be measured rather than
    asserted was, in this one field, displaying a constant.

    WHY 0.1.0-alpha. `22-Release-Plan` 5 defines the Alpha channel as "Unstable; data loss
    possible", tagged `0.Y.0-alpha.N`. That is a description of this software as the audit currently
    finds it, so it is what the version says. `1.0.0` claimed a stable release; the literal that
    preceded it in the UI claimed `1.0.0-beta.1`. Both were fiction, in the direction that flatters.

    VersionSuffix is overridable so a release build can drop it (`-p:VersionSuffix=`) or move the
    channel on (`-p:VersionSuffix=beta.1`) without editing this file. AssemblyVersion is pinned to
    the prefix only, deliberately: it participates in assembly binding, and a value that moves with
    every pre-release build would churn binding identity for no benefit.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>0.1.0</VersionPrefix>
    <VersionSuffix Condition="'$(VersionSuffix)' == ''">alpha.1</VersionSuffix>
    <AssemblyVersion>$(VersionPrefix).0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>$(VersionPrefix).0</FileVersion>
    <Product>Pine</Product>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
